File Types — Detailed Breakdown
The Core Lesson
File extensions are lies. The file is named Flag.pdf but isn't a PDF at all. In forensics, you never trust metadata — you trust the file magic bytes (the first few bytes that identify the actual format). The file command reads these magic bytes, not the extension.

The Compression Chain Explained
Each layer was chosen to test knowledge of a specific tool. Here's why each exists and what it does:

#	Format	Tool	Magic Bytes	Purpose
1	SHAR (shell archive)	sh / uudecode	#!/bin/sh	Unix shell script that recreates files from base64-encoded data. Ancient (1980s).
2	AR (archive)	ar	!<arch>\n	Binary archive format from GNU toolchain (used for .a static libraries).
3	CPIO	cpio	070701 / 070707	"Copy In/Out" — the original Unix archiver (1971). Still used in tar internally.
4	Bzip2	bzip2	BZh9	Block-sorting compressor (1996). Better ratio than gzip, slower.
5	Gzip	gzip	1f 8b	DEFLATE compression (1992). The internet standard.
6	Lzip	lzip	fd 37 7a 58 5a 00	LZMA-based, designed as a gzip replacement with better compression.
7	LZ4	lz4	04 22 4d 18	Extremely fast compression (2011). Sacrifices ratio for speed.
8	LZMA	xz / lzma	5d 00 00	The compression algorithm behind 7z. Best-in-class ratio.
9	LZOP	lzop	89 4c 4f	LZO-based. Fast, used in Linux kernel initramfs.
10	Lzip (again)	lzip	fd 37 7a 58 5a 00	Repeated to test if you'd blindly follow the pattern.
11	XZ	xz	fd 37 7a 58 5a 00	Container format for LZMA2 (the modern version of LZMA).
12	Hex text	xxd -r -p	ASCII hex pairs	Final encoding — the flag is hex-encoded to hide it in plain sight.

Why This Order?
The chain was designed to be progressively less familiar:

SHAR → AR → CPIO: Classic Unix archivers. Most CTF players know these.
Bzip2 → Gzip: Ubiquitous. Everyone uses these daily.
Lzip → LZ4 → LZMA → LZOP: Niche. These are the "gotcha" layers that waste time.
XZ → Hex: The finish.
The file Command — Your Best Friend
Under the hood, file reads a database (/etc/magic or /usr/share/file/magic) of known magic byte signatures. A few examples:

% PDF-1.x          → PDF
% PK\x03\x04       → ZIP
BZh9               → Bzip2
\x1f\x8b           → Gzip
\xfd7zXZ\x00       → XZ/Lzip
\x04\x22\x4d\x18   → LZ4
\x89LZO            → LZOP
!<arch>            → AR
070701            → CPIO (old ASCII format)

Why xxd -r -p at the End?
The final file contains a hex string like:

7069636f4354467b66316c656e406d335f6d316e3170756c407431306e5f663062326375723137795f37396230316332367d

xxd -r -p reverses hex encoding:

-r = reverse (decode instead of encode)
-p = postscript format (pure hex, no addresses)
This is the same encoding used in:

Memory dumps
Network packet captures
Hex editors
Certificate fingerprints
